# Clean Git Status When Done

After any task that touches the repo, **end with a clean `git status`** (no modified or untracked leftovers from your work). Do not leave the user to tidy up.

## Before you stop

1. Run `git status -sb`.
2. Clear every path you introduced or dirtied unless the user asked to keep it dirty.

## What to do with leftovers

| Leftover | Action |
| -------- | ------ |
| Intended product/docs change | Commit only if the user asked (or the ship/PR flow requires it); otherwise leave it only while still actively working that change |
| Empty or scratch `evidence/*`, temp scripts, `/tmp` copies in-repo | Delete |
| Unrelated edits you bumped into | `git restore` / discard |
| WIP the user may want later | `git stash push -u -m "wip: <short reason>"` and tell them the stash name |
| Merged feature/promote branch checked out | Switch back to `main` (or `staging` if that was the working base) and delete the local merged branch when safe |

## Do not

- End on a merged throwaway branch with a dirty tree.
- Leave empty evidence logs, aborted editor files, or half-applied patches.
- "Park" unrelated `.cursor/` or skill edits in the working tree — stash or commit them on purpose.

## Done means

```
git status -sb   # → clean on the intended branch (usually main)
```

If something must remain uncommitted, say so in one line and stash it — do not leave it as ambient dirt.
